<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Educational reconstruction of Weather.Theme.AppCompat / .Base.

  Session 3 rewrite. The previous version's own comment claimed it was a
  "Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.NoActionBar descendant", but the parent was actually
  Theme.AppCompat.NoActionBar - permanently light-mode AppCompat - with a dark gradient
  forced in as the window background. The result was that locations, search and settings
  were painted with the detail screen's dark palette regardless of the system setting, while
  reference-ui/locations.png shows the original rendering them as a light One UI surface on
  this same device.

  The original's chain:

      Weather.Theme.AppCompat.Base   parent=Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.NoActionBar
        android:statusBarColor      @color/status_bar_color
        android:navigationBarColor  @color/navigation_bar_color
        android:windowLightStatusBar @bool/window_light_status_bar
        colorPrimary                @color/weather_color_primary   (#387aff)
      Weather.Theme.AppCompat        + windowLightNavigationBar, windowLayoutInDisplayCutoutMode

  Everything that differs between light and dark is a RESOURCE with a values-night override -
  the bar colours, the surfaces, the boolean - so there is one theme, not two. That is worth
  preserving as the idiom, not just the result.

  ### No forced window background

  The original sets none here. The detail screen paints its own artwork over the window, and
  every other screen wants the system surface. Forcing a dark gradient was what made the
  light screens impossible.
-->
<resources>
    <style name="Study.Theme.AppCompat.Weather" parent="Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowLayoutInDisplayCutoutMode">shortEdges</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/study_oneui_background</item>
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">@bool/study_window_light_status_bar</item>
        <item name="colorPrimary">@color/study_oneui_accent</item>
        <item name="colorAccent">@color/study_oneui_accent</item>
        <item name="alertDialogTheme">@style/Study.OneUi.Dialog</item>
    </style>
</resources>
